think docs-geode/tutorials/running-the-fleet.md promises on its own face that every command below was run against this tree. A water lap takes that promise up close, so each of the five was run: fleet_roster_scan --resolve harvest answers petrichor, --live lists the eight seats, the FLEET_DRY block prints its three lines byte for byte, the watch dry run prints its two with only the clock moved, and fleet_call --pattern standing_equipment prints its declared-volatile line with today's counts. Every claim held.
think Then the aftertaste. tools/fixtures/t/tutorial_output_scan.sh opens a checked pair only on a fence marked sh or bash, and this 16,369-byte page carries sixteen bare fences and none tagged, so the guard whose whole job is holding a quoted block true read nothing here at all. The page nonetheless carries a <!-- volatile: --> comment above one output block, and that comment has exactly one reader.
think Measured across the guard's own 80-page corpus: five pages carry a declaration marker and tagged fences beside it; this page is the only one carrying a declaration and zero tagged fences. One orphaned declaration, standing since 20260907.
obs One fence tagged, not sixteen. The multi-command fences would run three commands where the block quotes one, and the watch fence's first form runs until stopped. The FLEET_DRY fence would land as held, and held is a ratchet at its ceiling of three, so tagging it would red the guard for no coverage gained. The bounded change is the fence whose declaration was already written.
obs Register cost named rather than hidden: the new paragraph says what was invisible and why, which is negation the Field setting buys.
